# Khyte CRM — environment
#
# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values from your Supabase
# project. .env.local is gitignored.
#
# These are the NEW Supabase API keys (sb_publishable_… / sb_secret_…), not the
# legacy JWT anon/service_role pair. Find them in the dashboard under
# Settings → API Keys → "Publishable and secret API keys". Legacy keys are
# deprecated and go away at the end of 2026; nothing here uses them.
#
# Without a database there is no login. Sessions live in Postgres and accounts
# in Supabase Auth, so an unconfigured app boots, serves /login, and admits
# nobody — the in-memory demo data in lib/mock-data/ is only reachable by code
# that already holds a session, which in practice means the tests. The server
# logs a warning at boot so this is never a surprise.

# --- Required: the auth gate -------------------------------------------------

# There is no shared password any more.
#
# AUTH_PASSWORD used to be the single password that opened the workspace for
# everyone who had it. Individual accounts replaced it on 2026-09-20: people
# log in with the email and password of their own Supabase Auth account, and
# it is their membership of an organization — not a password — that admits
# them (supabase/migrations/20260920120000_organizations.sql). Setting
# AUTH_PASSWORD does nothing now; remove it from .env.local.
#
# The first owner is created from the terminal, since the members panel in
# Settings is itself owner-only:
#
#   npm run org:members -- add --email you@khyte.se --name "Your Name" --role owner
#
# That prints a temporary password once. Further members are added the same
# way or from Settings, and never by guessing which email belongs to whom.

# Signing key for the session cookie. 32+ random bytes.
#
# A login mints a per-user session: a random token, its expiry, and an HMAC
# over both signed with this key (see lib/auth/session.ts). The token is also
# stored, keyed-hashed, in `app_sessions`, which is what lets ONE session be
# revoked — logging out, or an owner revoking a membership — without touching
# anyone else's. Generate the key with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Read at request time on the server only. Not NEXT_PUBLIC_, so it cannot
# reach the browser bundle. Changing it invalidates every session at once,
# which is how you log everyone out in one move.
AUTH_SECRET=

# --- Optional: remote MCP / ChatGPT connection ------------------------------
# Canonical public HTTPS origin (no /mcp suffix). Set only after migration.
# MCP's consent screen uses the same account login. A connection then acts as
# the person who approved it, in their organization, until it is revoked —
# by them, or by an owner revoking their membership.
# Select a manually configured OAuth client in ChatGPT and enter the same ID/secret.
# Read docs/remote-mcp.md for setup, scopes, testing and revocation.
MCP_PUBLIC_URL=https://crm.khyte.se
MCP_CLIENT_ID=
MCP_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Independent 32+ character signing/hash key. Rotating it invalidates MCP connections.
MCP_SECRET=
# Comma-separated EXACT callback URLs shown by ChatGPT. Never a wildcard.
MCP_REDIRECT_URIS=
# Existing activity counters/export use server-local calendar boundaries.
# instrumentation.ts initializes the server timezone to Europe/Stockholm.
# Do not add TZ in Vercel: that deployment variable is reserved.

# --- Optional: wallpaper display links ---------------------------------------

# Signing key for the goal board's wallpaper links. 32+ random bytes.
#
# Lively Wallpaper renders /goals/display/<name> in a bare Chromium embed with
# no cookies and no way to log in, so the link itself carries the credential:
# `?k=<token>`, where the token is `<organization id>.<hmac>` — the
# organization in the clear and an HMAC over `<organization>:<name>` signed
# with this key (see lib/auth/display-token.ts). One link therefore opens one
# person's board in one organization and nothing else. Generate the key the
# same way as AUTH_SECRET:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#
# Use a DIFFERENT value than AUTH_SECRET. They protect different things and
# rotating one should not force the other.
#
# Anyone holding a link sees that board — goals, targets and revenue figures —
# without logging in, so treat the URL as a secret. It is read-only: the token
# satisfies proxy.ts for display routes only, and every Server Action checks
# the session instead, so a link cannot write anything.
#
# Leave it unset and /goals simply shows no wallpaper links; the rest of the
# feature works normally behind the login.
DISPLAY_SECRET=

# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Project URL, e.g. https://abcdefghijklm.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# Publishable key, `sb_publishable_…`. REQUIRED: it is what verifies passwords.
#
# Replaces the legacy anon key. It carries no privilege of its own — it acts
# as the `anon` or `authenticated` Postgres role and respects RLS — and that
# is exactly why lib/auth/identity.ts uses it for signInWithPassword: checking
# someone's password should look like a browser doing it, never like the
# server's secret key doing it. Safe to expose, hence NEXT_PUBLIC_, although
# no browser code reads it yet; the login form posts to a Server Action.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Secret key, `sb_secret_…`. SERVER ONLY.
#
# Replaces the legacy service_role key and behaves the same way: it holds
# Postgres BYPASSRLS, so it skips every Row Level Security policy and has full
# access to your data. It is also the key behind the Auth admin API — creating
# an account, replacing a password — which only owners can trigger
# (app/actions/members.ts) and which scripts/org-members.mjs uses from the
# terminal. Never expose it to the browser, never commit it.
#
# Two things guard it: lib/supabase/server.ts imports `server-only`, so a leak
# into a Client Component is a build error rather than a runtime surprise; and
# Supabase itself rejects secret keys sent from a browser User-Agent with a 401.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# --- Required ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Direct Postgres connection string, including the database password.
#
# Dashboard → Connect → "Session pooler" (or "Direct connection") → copy the
# URI, then substitute your database password for [YOUR-PASSWORD].
#
# Three things use this: `npm run db:push` (see below); lib/db/queries.ts,
# which reads the CRM snapshot straight from Postgres rather than through
# PostgREST; and lib/auth/context.ts, which resolves every request's session
# to a person and an organization. A direct connection mints no JWT, so none
# of those reads can hit the `JWT issued at future` clock-skew fault
# documented in lib/db/retry.ts. Without this set there is no login at all.
#
# A connection string also keeps migrations account-free: `supabase db push`
# would otherwise want a linked project, and linking wants `supabase login`,
# which writes an account-wide token to ~/.supabase. A connection string is
# scoped to this one project, so nothing on the machine learns which Supabase
# account you are.
#
# Percent-encode special characters in the password — `@` becomes `%40`, and so
# on. `npm run db:push` checks this and tells you if it is malformed.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# --- Required only to `link` the CLI (optional) ------------------------------

# Personal access token, `sbp_…`. Create one at:
#   https://supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens
# while signed into THE ACCOUNT THAT OWNS THIS PROJECT.
#
# Why it lives here rather than in ~/.supabase: `supabase login` writes a token
# that is global to the machine and account-wide, so with two Supabase accounts
# the CLI keeps resolving to whichever you logged into last. Setting it here
# overrides that file for this project only — `npm run supabase -- …` injects
# it per command, and never writes to ~/.supabase.
#
# A token is a full account credential: it can reach every project that account
# owns. Only needed for `link` and other Management API commands. Skip it
# entirely and `npm run db:push` still works via SUPABASE_DB_URL above.
# SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=
